@meta
id: 09-15
title: Delete a method with a paragraph jump
chapter: 09
type: lesson
skills: op-linewise, op-motion
par: 12
minutes: 5
vscode: select four lines carefully, Delete, then fix the blank lines
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
`legacyRefund` has been unused since the payment service rewrite. Take it
out of the provider.

The method goes, and so does one of the blank lines around it — the file
should end up with exactly one blank line between `findOne` and `cancel`,
like every other gap in the file. Nothing else changes.

@keys
| `}` | forward to the next blank line |
| `{` | back to the previous blank line |
| `d}` | delete from here to the next blank line |
| `4j` | four lines down |

@hints
In a code file, blank lines are the structure. A method with blank lines
either side of it is a paragraph, and you have a motion for paragraphs.
--
`d}` deletes from the cursor to the next blank line. Start it *on* the
blank line above the method and the leading gap goes too.
--
`4j` lands on the blank line above `legacyRefund`. Then `d}`.

@solution
    4j d} :wq<Enter>

Eight keystrokes for a four-line method, with no counting and no
selecting. Count the lines instead and you get it wrong the day someone
adds a line to the method.

Starting on the blank line rather than on the `public` line is the whole
trick. `}` moves to the *next* blank line, so from the gap above the
method it runs to the gap below it, and the delete takes the method plus
one blank line — leaving the other one as the separator you wanted. Start
on the `public` line and you would delete the method but keep both gaps,
and be left with a double blank line to tidy.

There is a subtlety underneath that is worth knowing about. `}` is a
charwise motion, but when it starts in the first column and ends in the
first column, vim promotes the whole thing to linewise — which is why you
get clean whole lines here rather than a half-joined mess. The rule shows
up with `{`, `(` and `)` too.

`d}` is the everyday way to remove a block of code in vim before you know
text objects. Once you have `dap` from chapter 10 you will use that
instead for paragraphs, but `d}` needs nothing but what you already have,
and `y}` and `c}` fall out of it for free.

@start order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async findOne(id: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });
  }

  public async legacyRefund(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    return this.paymentService.refundLegacy(order.id, order.total);
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'CANCELLED' } });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async findOne(id: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'CANCELLED' } });
  }
}
